WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.776
Human performance is the ability to do meaningful work reliably under real conditions.

00:00:05.776 --> 00:00:11.753
It is not the same as being busy, staying online all day, or pushing through every limit.

00:00:11.753 --> 00:00:22.901
It is the practical capacity to think clearly, act skillfully, communicate well, recover appropriately, and produce useful outcomes when the environment is imperfect.

00:00:22.901 --> 00:00:29.012
Performance is shaped by physical, cognitive, emotional, environmental, and social factors.

00:00:29.012 --> 00:00:38.616
Sleep, workload, stress, workspace setup, team communication, task clarity, tools, habits, and recovery all influence how people actually work.

00:00:38.616 --> 00:00:49.361
A person may have strong motivation and still perform poorly if the system around them creates constant interruption, unclear priorities, or unsustainable pace.

00:00:49.361 --> 00:00:52.585
Performance is also different from productivity.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:01:00.577
Productivity often focuses on output volume: how many tickets closed, words written, calls handled, or tasks completed.

00:01:00.577 --> 00:01:08.032
Performance asks a broader question: was the work correct, resilient, sustainable, and appropriate to the risk?

00:01:08.032 --> 00:01:15.016
In technical, operational, creative, and leadership roles, quality and judgment matter as much as speed.

00:01:15.016 --> 00:01:22.605
The core idea of this course is that human performance is a system design problem, not just a motivation problem.

00:01:22.605 --> 00:01:27.507
Unlimited focus, unlimited willpower, and constant peak output are myths.

00:01:27.507 --> 00:01:41.006
Strong performers build conditions that make good work repeatable: clear priorities, protected attention, recovery, feedback, practice, decision supports, and team norms that reduce avoidable friction.

00:01:41.006 --> 00:01:45.036
This systems view is useful because it creates more options.

00:01:45.036 --> 00:01:59.542
Instead of asking people to simply try harder, leaders and individuals can adjust the workload, clarify the goal, change the environment, add a checklist, improve handoffs, or create recovery time after intense work.

00:01:59.542 --> 00:02:05.452
Human performance improves when the design of work respects how people actually operate.
